  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on synthetic window-view images generated from 3D models rather than actual photographs taken from inside the physical rental units.",
      "what": "This study developed a pipeline to estimate Housing Window View Desirability (HWVD) in Tokyo by combining photorealistic synthetic window-view images generated from Google 3D Tiles with a hedonic price model. Using rental records for three-bedroom properties, the model isolates the rent premium attributable to window views after controlling for structural and locational factors, which improved out-of-sample rent prediction R-squared from 0.62 to 0.71. Semantic analysis of the views revealed that higher desirability scores correlate positively with visible greenness and negatively with building views, with the highest scores clustered in wealthier southwestern wards like Shinagawa-ku and Minato-ku.",
      "why": "It establishes a scalable, automated method to quantify and map the economic value of residential window views across an entire metropolitan area, revealing a stark socio-economic disparity in access to high-quality visual landscapes."
    }
  },
